body:
## Problem

`lino-env` does not parse multi-line quoted values. When a key's value is opened with `"` (or `'`) and closed on a later line, `lino-env` reads only the first line and captures the literal opening quote as the value, losing the rest of the content.

## Reproduction

`.lenv`:
```
HIVE_TELEGRAM_BOT_CONFIGURATION: "
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN: 'xxx'
TELEGRAM_ALLOWED_CHATS:
  -1002975819706
TELEGRAM_BOT_VERBOSE: true
"
```

Script:
```js
const { LinoEnv } = await use('lino-arguments');
const lenv = new LinoEnv('.lenv');
lenv.read();
console.log(JSON.stringify(lenv.get('HIVE_TELEGRAM_BOT_CONFIGURATION')));
// Prints: "\""
// Expected: the full multi-line YAML-like block between the quotes
```

## Expected behavior

When a value starts with `"` or `'` and the closing quote is on a subsequent line, `lino-env` should capture everything between the opening and closing quote (including newlines) as a single string value.

This is the behavior already specified and tested in the parent `links-notation` spec:
https://github.com/link-foundation/links-notation/blob/main/js/tests/MultilineQuotedString.test.js

## Why this matters

Multi-line quoted values are useful for embedding structured configuration blobs (YAML, JSON, shell scripts, nested lino) inside a single `.lenv` entry without having to shell-escape newlines. Today, users have to fall back to parsing `.lenv` manually with a regex, which defeats the purpose of using `lino-env`.

## Workaround currently in use

```js
const readMultilineQuoted = (filePath, key) => {
  const text = fs.readFileSync(filePath, 'utf8');
  const re = new RegExp(`^${key}:\\s*(["'])([\\s\\S]*?)\\1`, 'm');
  const m = text.match(re);
  return m ? m[2] : null;
};
```
